We introduce a modest negative weight on position for the left action (params[0] = -1.0) so that when the car is far left (negative position) the left‑push score rises, encouraging early left accelerations that build momentum. The velocity weight for the left action is slightly reduced (params[1] = -5.5) to make the left decision less aggressive at high speeds. The right‑action weights remain strong (6,6) to dominate once sufficient positive position/velocity is achieved. The no‑acceleration row gets a slightly larger velocity coefficient (params[3] = 5.4) and a less negative bias (params[7] = -5.0) to provide a smoother transition from the left‑push phase to the right‑push phase. Biases for left and right actions stay neutral (0) so they only influence decisions through state‑dependent terms. This combination aims to balance the oscillatory “rock‑back” momentum‑building with timely right pushes, targeting a return around the expected optimum (~‑120 steps).
